# Two shell gates that exited 1 without saying why (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` was found dying before it printed its verdict, because a bare
`name="$(pipeline)"` assignment inherits the pipeline's status and `set -euo pipefail` ends
the script on it. The same shape was then looked for in the other shell scripts that report
something. Two of them had it, and in both the code that should have caught the problem was
written on the line directly after the one that died. Details of the runner case are in
`gate-runner-only-and-env-2026-10-04.log`.

Both defects are in the "the gate knows and says nothing" class: the exit code was already
correct, so nothing upstream complained, and only the words were missing. Captured output is
quoted verbatim, including its own punctuation, and test names are kept on one line each.

== 1. scripts/ci/check-versions.sh could not report an empty optionalDependencies

The gate compares the Cargo package version against six npm package versions and, in check 5,
compares the `optionalDependencies` names in `npm/chaos/package.json` against the platform
package directories on disk. `release.yml` resolves the release version from that file and
stamps the platform packages from it, so an emptied `optionalDependencies` is the breakage
check 5 is specific about. Drive it in a copy of the tree with the field deleted:

$ node -e 'const fs=require("fs");const p="crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/chaos/package.json";
    const j=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(p,"utf8"));delete j.optionalDependencies;
    fs.writeFileSync(p,JSON.stringify(j,null,2)+"\n")'
$ bash scripts/ci/check-versions.sh > out.txt 2> err.txt
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1
$ cat out.txt
    check-versions: Cargo crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-bin/Cargo.toml -> 0.4.2
$ wc -c < err.txt
    0

Against the same tree with the field present, for contrast, the baseline is green:

$ bash scripts/ci/check-versions.sh
    check-versions: Cargo crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-bin/Cargo.toml -> 0.4.2
    check-versions: OK — Cargo and all npm packages agree on 0.4.2

The cause was `declared_names="$(grep -v '^$' <<<"$declared" | cut -d' ' -f1 | sort)"`. `grep`
exits 1 when no line is left to print, and an empty declared set is precisely the state where
nothing is left to print. Check 4 upstream of it had already passed silently, because its loop
runs once per declared entry and there were none.

The fix removes the failure mode rather than suppressing its status. `sed '/^$/d'` deletes
empty lines and has no "no lines matched" exit status, so the comparison downstream runs, and
the empty side of the comparison is now labelled in words:

$ bash scripts/ci/check-versions.sh
    check-versions: Cargo crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-bin/Cargo.toml -> 0.4.2
    check-versions: MISMATCH platform package set
      optionalDependencies names:
      (none declared)
      package.json names on disk:
    chaos-code-darwin-arm64
    chaos-code-darwin-x64
    chaos-code-linux-arm64
    chaos-code-linux-x64
    chaos-code-win32-arm64
    chaos-code-win32-x64
    check-versions: FAILED

== 2. scripts/ci/test-check-versions.py, 6 cases, 2 of which fail without the fix

The fixture copies the gate, `Cargo.toml` and the npm tree into a temporary directory and runs
the copied script, so the mutations land on the copy and the repository is never touched.
Every failing case asserts on stderr as well as the exit code, because the exit code was
already 1 while the gate was broken.

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-versions.py
    test_cargo_version_drift_is_named (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok
    test_faithful_copy_passes (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok
    test_one_dropped_pin_names_the_package (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok
    test_optional_dependencies_empty_object_reports_too (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok
    test_optional_dependencies_removed_reports_the_package_set (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok
    test_platform_package_version_drift_is_named (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... ok

    Ran 6 tests in 1.348s

    OK

Against the pre-fix gate, exactly the two empty-set cases fail. The other four stay green,
which is what shows they are pinning something else (the version comparison, the non-empty set
comparison) rather than being satisfied by the fix:

    test_optional_dependencies_empty_object_reports_too (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... FAIL
    test_optional_dependencies_removed_reports_the_package_set (__main__.CheckVersionsReportTests) ... FAIL

    Ran 6 tests in 1.368s

    FAILED (failures=2)

The mutated file was restored with a plain `cp` and verified with `cmp`.

== 3. scripts/install.sh died inside the mirror loop it exists to run

`download_github` tries each candidate URL, refuses a body smaller than `min_bytes` or an HTML
error page served with status 200, moves to the next candidate, and if all of them fail prints
one `why:` line per distinct reason plus the `CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR` tip. Its size probe was:

    size="$(wc -c < "$dest" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]')"
    [[ -n "$size" ]] || size=0

The `2>/dev/null` and the `|| size=0` on the next line both say the author expected `wc` to be
able to fail. It cannot: the failing substitution ends the script first, so the fallback is
unreachable, and the loop whose whole job is to fall back across mirrors dies part-way through
it, with no reason printed.

Driven by extracting the shipped function verbatim into a harness, against a local HTTP
endpoint on 127.0.0.1 serving a 4 KiB body, with a `wc` that exits 1 stood in front of `PATH`
to mimic an unreadable body. The only stub is the candidate list, because the real one
resolves github.com.

Healthy control, same function, real `wc`:

    CASE=healthy-fixed EXIT=0 dest_exists=4096
    http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin
      try: http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin

Broken `wc`, the function as it now stands:

    CASE=broken-fixed EXIT=1 dest_exists=no
      try: http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin
      try: http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin?mirror=1
    error: download failed for http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin
      why: too small (0 bytes) from http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin
      why: too small (0 bytes) from http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin?mirror=1
      last: too small (0 bytes) from http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin?mirror=1
      tip: set CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR=https://ghfast.top  or  CHAOS_CN=1

Broken `wc`, the function at `HEAD` before the fix. Both candidates were offered; one `try:`
line appeared; there was no `error:` line and no reason:

    CASE=broken-before EXIT=1 dest_exists=no
      try: http://127.0.0.1:8099/artifact.bin

== 4. scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py, 5 cases, 1 of which fails without the fix

The same harness, as a fixture: a threaded `http.server` on an ephemeral port, the function
extracted from `install.sh` by its own closing brace at column 0, and the four reasons the
loop can produce, so the file is not proving only the branch it was written for.

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py
    test_full_body_is_accepted_and_the_url_is_reported (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... ok
    test_html_error_page_is_refused_by_name (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... ok
    test_missing_object_reports_the_status_code (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... ok
    test_short_body_reports_the_size_it_measured (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... ok
    test_unreadable_size_reports_a_reason_instead_of_dying (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... ok

    Ran 5 tests in 2.643s

    OK

Against the pre-fix `install.sh`, only the case named for the defect fails, and the three
cases that share the same report path stay green, so `too small (0 bytes)` is not a constant
the fixture could be reading from its own setup:

    test_unreadable_size_reports_a_reason_instead_of_dying (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests) ... FAIL

    Ran 5 tests in 2.607s

    FAILED (failures=1)

Restored with `cp`, verified with `cmp`, and `git diff --stat scripts/install.sh` afterwards
showed only the intended 7 added and 1 removed lines.

== 5. How much of the shape is left, and what the scan misses

The line-based scan for it is this. It reports 142 single-line command substitutions assigned
to a plain variable, of which 9 have a pipe inside and no `|| true` to absorb the status:

$ git grep -nE '^[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="\$\(' -- '*.sh' | wc -l
    142

$ git grep -nE '^[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="\$\([^"]*\|' -- '*.sh' | grep -v '|| true'
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/scripts/install-enterprise.sh:164:    sysctl_bin="$(command -v sysctl || echo /usr/sbin/sysctl)"
    crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/scripts/install.sh:193:    sysctl_bin="$(command -v sysctl || echo /usr/sbin/sysctl)"
    scripts/install.sh:175:  os="$(uname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:549:remote_digest="$(on_host 'sha256sum /lab/workspace/notes.txt' | cut -c1-64)"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:550:fetched_digest="$(on_dev 'sha256sum /lab/local/fetched.txt' | cut -c1-64)"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:995:tokens_before="$(on_dev 'wc -l </shared/token' | tr -d ' ')"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:1013:tokens_after="$(on_dev 'wc -l </shared/token' | tr -d ' ')"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:1337:fetched_digest="$(on_dev 'sha256sum /lab/local/through-the-forward.bin' | cut -c1-64)"
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:1338:served_digest="$(on_host 'sha256sum /lab/service/payload.bin' | cut -c1-64)"

All nine were read and left alone. The two `sysctl_bin` lines end in `|| echo`, so the status
assigned is 0 whatever `command -v` answers; and both of those files run `set -e` without
`pipefail` at script scope (one sets it inside a single subshell elsewhere), so a middle
stage's status would not reach the assignment either. `os="$(uname -s | tr ...)"` runs before
anything exists to report, and a host that cannot answer `uname` should stop. The six in
`scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh` compare a `sha256sum` computed on one side of a
tunnel against one computed on the other: if either fails, the equality assertion below them
is meaningless, and stopping there is the correct reading of the script.

The scan is a floor, not a census, and its blind spot is the reason this file exists at all:
the expression `[^"]*` stops at the first quote, so a substitution that quotes something
before its pipe is invisible to it. That is how both fixed lines escaped it,
`check-versions.sh` with `<<<"$declared"` and `install.sh` with `"$dest"`. The two were found
by reading the runner defect's shape into the other reporting scripts, not by the scan.

== 6. Wiring and reproduction

Both fixtures run in both roots, on the executable lines rather than in prose, which is what
`check-guard-wiring.py` requires. The container image builds `nodejs` and `curl`, so neither
fixture silently skips there; the run below shows 6 and 5 tests actually executing inside it:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "version lockstep" --only "installer guards"
    == version lockstep
    Ran 6 tests in 3.885s
    OK
    check-versions: OK — Cargo and all npm packages agree on 0.4.2
    7 check(s), 0 failure(s)
    == installer guards
    Ran 14 tests in 0.329s
    Ran 8 tests in 0.002s
    Ran 5 tests in 2.657s
    OK

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 2 of 32 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-versions.py
$ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (59 files in scripts/ci/, 58 reachable, 54 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
